※ pl4g-test: compile=fail
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4933 LANG_MARK_NOT_NEEDED

※ `@[impure]` warns whoever reads or calls a function that it changes something
※ of the program's that outlives the call.  Written where the body changes
※ nothing -- it only works out an answer -- the warning is false, and a reader
※ who learns that stops heeding it, so it is refused.  `main` is marked only
※ because it calls `next`, which justifies nothing, so it is refused as well.
※ A function whose calls are to be kept though they change nothing says
※ `@[opaque]`.

let std := ⎕import("std")

@[impure]
fn next(a: u8) → u8:
    a + 1u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    if next(1u8) = 2u8: 0u6 else: 1u6
